@NODE REQUIREMENTS "System Requirements"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}Minimum system@{ub}

FriendSh3ep runs on @{b}AmigaOS 3.2.x@{ub} or compatible, on a @{b}68030 50MHz@{ub} or better. A @{b}68060@{ub} (or a fast 68040) is recommended for a noticeably smoother experience -- fonts, emoji and images are all decoded live.

@{b}Required@{ub}
@{lindent 2}
1. @{b}Fast, working Internet access@{ub} -- Ethernet strongly recommended. Mastodon timelines, images and emoji are network-hungry; dial-up/serial connections will struggle.
2. @{b}AmiSSL v5@{ub} -- every Mastodon server speaks HTTPS only.
3. The @{b}EmojiGear v6@{ub} font & emoji package -- provides the TrueType/OpenType fonts and colour emoji FriendSh3ep uses by default (see @{" Themes & Styles " LINK THEMES}).
@{lindent 0}
@{b}Optional, for a better experience@{ub}
@{lindent 2}
1. The @{b}OpenURL@{ub} library, plus a web browser -- lets you jump straight to links and attached URLs instead of just reading them.
2. @{b}AHI@{ub} and @{b}mpega.library@{ub} -- needed to play back attached audio.
3. A WebP picture datatype: your OS install may be bundled with GIF, PNG and JPEG, but WebP may be missing. FriendSh3ep relies on picture datatypes to decode images, so you may miss images without it.
@{lindent 0}
@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE


@NODE MASTODON "Mastodon, the Fediverse & Your Accounts"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}What is Mastodon?@{ub}

Mastodon is a social network, but unlike most others it isn't run by one company from one web site. It's made of thousands of independently run @{b}servers@{ub} (also called @{i}instances@{ui}), each with its own address -- mastodon.social, mas.to, piaille.fr, and so on -- all talking to each other over a shared, open protocol. This network of servers is what people call the @{b}Fediverse@{ub}. An account on any server can follow, reply to and boost (share) accounts on any other server, exactly as if they were on the same one.

Each server has its own rules of moderation. A good server should update its Mastodon code quickly, admins should communicate, and malevolent servers should be blocked. Yet some servers would block "Twitter and Bluesky" mirror servers, by choice, or not. Servers are thematized or not, allow any languages, or not. The number of characters per toot, and the number of attached images and videos varies: think about it when you choose a server.

You may create accounts on many servers, by themes: this is common practice, and FriendSh3ep allows you to switch servers and accounts with one click.

Also, the important thing about Mastodon servers is that you can "move" your account and its content from one server to another, which you may do because the rules do not fit you or the server is not maintained anymore.

There is no such thing as a "FriendSh3ep account": FriendSh3ep is only a @{i}client@{ui}. You create your account on a server's own web site first (pick any server you like -- they're all part of the same Fediverse), and only then tell FriendSh3ep to connect to it. (Note you can also connect anonymously to some servers.)

@{b}Connecting an account@{ub}

Click the @{b}Accounts@{ub} button in the title bar (or @{b}FriendSh3ep > Accounts...@{ub} in the menu) to open the Login window:
@{lindent 2}
1. Type your server's address in @{b}Server@{ub} and click @{b}Login@{ub}.
2. FriendSh3ep asks the server for an authorization page and shows its address. Open that address in your web browser, log in and approve FriendSh3ep there -- the server then gives you a short one-time @{b}code@{ub}.
3. Paste that code into the @{b}Code@{ub} field and click @{b}Submit Code@{ub}.
@{lindent 0}
FriendSh3ep never sees your password: only a revocable token handed to it by the server after you approve it in your own browser. The account is then remembered for next time.

@{b}Multiple accounts@{ub}

Every account you've connected -- on any server -- appears as a row in the accounts list at the bottom of the Login window. Click a row to switch to that account instantly; the @{b}Delete Server@{ub} button forgets the selected one. Switching accounts is a very Mastodon thing to do, and FriendSh3ep makes it a single click.

@{b}Browsing anonymously@{ub}

You don't need an account at all to look around. Click @{b}Anonymous@{ub} instead of logging in, and FriendSh3ep connects read-only to whatever server is currently in the @{b}Server@{ub} field -- no token, no browser round-trip. Anonymous browsing is limited to the @{b}Local@{ub} and @{b}Federated@{ub} timelines (see @{" Timeline Views " LINK VIEWMODES}): no posting, no Home, no Notifications, since those need a real account.

To make this easy on first launch, the accounts list already comes pre-filled with a handful of servers confirmed to allow anonymous timeline browsing -- just pick one and go, or type in any server of your own choosing.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE


@NODE VIEWMODES "Timeline Views"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}The 8 Timeline Views@{ub}

The row of view buttons switches what the main toot timeline is showing. Each one is its own independent, remembered scroll position.

@IMAGE PROGDIR:friendsh3ep_viewmodes.iff
@{lindent 2}
@{b}User@{ub}@{lindent 4}
Your own posts (needs a real, logged-in account).@{lindent 2}

@{b}Home@{ub}@{lindent 4}
Friends & their re-toots -- everyone you follow. They can be on any other server.@{lindent 2}

@{b}Local@{ub}@{lindent 4}
Your server & your server users' re-toots -- everyone posting on the same server as the current account (works anonymously too). Boosted toots can originate from other servers.@{lindent 2}

@{b}Federated@{ub}@{lindent 4}
Around the world -- everything your server currently knows about from the rest of the Fediverse (works anonymously too). This is very different from server to server: there are more toots, and more diverse toots displayed.@{lindent 2}

@{b}Search@{ub}@{lindent 4}
Word and hashtag search, and account search -- see also the profile pages this view can open (subscribe/unsubscribe, etc).@{lindent 2}

@{b}Notifications@{ub}@{lindent 4}
Favourites, boosts, replies, follows and mentions concerning you.@{lindent 2}

@{b}Bookmarks@{ub}@{lindent 4}
You'll find the toots you bookmarked here. Note that bookmarked toots data are kept in a special cache under the user directory, per account, not in the volatile cache, and you can check them offline.@{lindent 2}

@{b}News@{ub}@{lindent 4}
Servers collect the most shared news web pages from news sites, from the last couple of days, and sort them for you. This list changes on a daily basis.
@{lindent 0}
A @{b}Refresh@{ub} button next to the view buttons reloads the current view.

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE


@NODE COMPOSE "Writing a Toot"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}Composing@{ub}

Click the @{b}Toot+@{ub} button in the title bar, or use @{b}FriendSh3ep > New Toot...@{ub} (Amiga-T), to open the Toot window. The same window is reused for replying to, quoting, messaging or modifying a toot -- the title line at the top always says which one you're doing.

Type your text in the main text area; a live counter under it shows how many characters you've used against the server's own maximum.

@{b}Visibility@{ub}

A chooser sets who can see the toot:
@{lindent 2}
@{b}Public@{ub}@{lindent 4}
Visible to everyone, shown in public timelines.@{lindent 2}
@{b}Public (Quiet)@{ub}@{lindent 4}
Visible to everyone, but hidden from public timelines.@{lindent 2}
@{b}Followers@{ub}@{lindent 4}
Visible only to your followers.@{lindent 2}
@{b}Private mention@{ub}@{lindent 4}
Visible only to @mentioned accounts -- like a PM.
@{lindent 0}
A second chooser, @{b}who can quote you@{ub}, offers @{b}Everybody@{ub}, @{b}Followers only@{ub} or @{b}Me only@{ub}. A line under both choosers always explains in plain words what the current combination means.

@{b}Attaching a file@{ub}

The @{b}Attach Media@{ub} row lets you pick one file to attach, using the usual ASL file requester (or type the path directly). Supported types are:
@{lindent 2}
Images: gif, jpg / jpeg, png
Audio / video: mp3, ogg, mpg, mp4
@{lindent 0}
Click the @{b}X@{ub} button next to it to clear the attachment before sending.

@{b}Emoji@{ub}

Click the smiley button in the Toot window to open the @{b}Emoji Box@{ub} -- the same one used everywhere else in FriendSh3ep -- and insert emoji by clicking them, or by their assigned F-keys while the text area has focus.

When you're ready, click @{b}Toot@{ub} (the button's label follows what you're doing: @{b}Reply@{ub}, @{b}Quote@{ub}, @{b}Modify@{ub}...).

@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE


@NODE PLAYSETTINGS "Play Mode & Settings"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}Autoscroll Play@{ub}

Instead of scrolling by hand, let FriendSh3ep advance through the timeline for you:
@{lindent 2}
@{b}Space@{ub}@{lindent 4}
Next toot -- smoothly scrolls to reveal the next one. While Play mode is running, Space instead stops it.@{lindent 2}
@{b}P@{ub}@{lindent 4}
Start Autoscroll Play: moves to the next toot on its own, every few seconds, for as long as you leave it running.@{lindent 2}
@{b}U@{ub}@{lindent 4}
Move to the top of the current timeline.
@{lindent 0}
These are also in the @{b}Timeline@{ub} menu.

@{b}Settings window@{ub}

Open it from @{b}Settings > General Settings@{ub} (Amiga-P). It's a tabbed window; changes apply immediately and are saved when you close it.

@IMAGE PROGDIR:friendsh3ep_settings.iff
@{lindent 2}
@{u}Tab "User experience"@{uu}@{lindent 4}
@{b}Play toot time (seconds)@{ub}@{lindent 6}
How long Autoscroll Play waits on each toot before advancing (3-60s).@{lindent 4}
@{b}Smooth auto scroll@{ub}@{lindent 6}
On: the eased, animated scroll used by Space/Play mode above. Off: jumps straight to the next toot instead -- lighter on slower machines.@{lindent 4}
@{b}URL Link Action@{ub}@{lindent 6}
What clicking a link does: Ask, Use OpenURL, or Copy to Clipboard.@{lindent 4}
@{b}Direct download .zip,.lha@{ub}@{lindent 6}
and the @{b}Download directory@{ub} used for it.@{lindent 2}

@{u}Tab "Paths & Cache"@{uu}@{lindent 4}
Cache and user-data directories, maximum cache size, whether to keep big user icons/thumbnails on disk, and a @{b}Flush cache@{ub} button.@{lindent 2}

@{u}Tab "Thumbnails & icons"@{uu}@{lindent 4}
Bigger/minified thumbnails, and the scaling quality and RGB draw function used to resize images -- tune these down on a plain 68020 or a native AGA screen, up on faster machines and RTG screens.
@{lindent 0}
@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE


@NODE THEMES "Themes & Styles"
@TOC MAIN

@{b}Fonts & Theme window@{ub}

Open it from @{b}Settings > Theme Settings@{ub}.

@IMAGE PROGDIR:friendsh3ep_theme.iff
@{lindent 2}
@{u}Fonts@{uu}@{lindent 4}
@{b}Primary font@{ub}, @{b}Fallback font 1/2@{ub}: TrueType/OpenType (.ttf/.otf) fonts tried in order, so uncommon scripts still display something sensible even when the primary font lacks the glyph.
@{b}UI emoji font@{ub}: a plain two-colour glyph font for buttons/navbar.
@{b}Color emoji font@{ub}: a CBDT/CBLC colour bitmap font (e.g. NotoColorEmoji.ttf) for full-colour emoji in the timeline.@{lindent 2}

@{u}Options@{uu}@{lindent 4}
@{b}Antialias when possible@{ub} and @{b}Emoji quality scaling@{ub} -- both trade a bit of speed/Chip RAM for smoother-looking text and emoji. In 8 bit mode, antialias can have problems with the background of buttons, so 8 bit indexed palette Workbench users may not want to use it.@{lindent 2}

@{u}Presets@{uu}@{lindent 4}
@{b}Low quality@{ub}, @{b}High quality@{ub}, @{b}Monospace@{ub} -- one-click combinations of the options above for common setups (plain AGA screen, fast RTG machine, code-style monospaced reading).@{lindent 2}

@{u}Theme@{uu}@{lindent 4}
A theme is a subdirectory of @{b}themes/@{ub} bundled with FriendSh3ep (or added by you) holding a @{b}style.txt@{ub} plus its own images: skins for buttons, title bar, colours, and so on. Pick one from the @{b}Theme@{ub} chooser, or "-" for the plain built-in look. @{b}Scan Themes@{ub} rescans the themes directory if you've just added a new one.
@{lindent 0}
@{" Back to Contents " LINK MAIN}
@ENDNODE
